WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.374
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Looking to
host your own website, blog,

00:00:02.374 --> 00:00:03.539
or e-commerce site?

00:00:03.539 --> 00:00:05.580
Today we'll be introducing
you to the lab running

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.370
WordPress on App Engine
flexible environment.

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:10.470
In this lab, you'll get
to set up and recognize

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.740
all the benefits of WordPress
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.079
WordPress is one of the most
popular open-source content

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:17.910
management systems
and GCP can help

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:20.869
you run your website in an
effective and scalable manner.

00:00:20.869 --> 00:00:22.410
By using App Engine,
we can make sure

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:24.785
that your website scales to
any number of viewers quickly

00:00:24.785 --> 00:00:26.220
and automatically.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.590
Google Cloud SQL and
Google Cloud Storage

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:30.330
will let you host your
database and files

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.866
using managed
infrastructure, so you

00:00:31.866 --> 00:00:33.990
don't need to worry about
resizing virtual machines

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:36.300
or emergency downtime requests.

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.670
Basic knowledge of PHP
and Linux text editors

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:41.430
will be required to finish
this lab on QwikLabs.

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:43.410
First in this lab,
you'll make sure all

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.300
the appropriate
services are enabled,

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.460
then you'll set up App
Engine and Cloud SQL

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.500
to host the website and
database separately.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:51.000
You'll also use
Google Cloud Storage

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.344
to host your media library.

00:00:52.344 --> 00:00:53.760
At the end of the
lab, you'll know

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.540
how to fully deploy a
WordPress website on GCP.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:00.390
You'll be using a student or GCP
account to work on these labs,

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:02.620
but if you want to get
started with your own account,

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:04.980
here's $300 to get you started.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.920
Today, I'll answer a question
from Vinayak Hedge on YouTube.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.570
He asks, don't you have any
tutorials for GCP IRT core?

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:14.190
I am trying to send data
from my Arduino to GCP

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:16.260
but I am unable to do
it as I am not aware

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.570
how to send data using MQTT.

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:19.435
Great question.

00:01:19.435 --> 00:01:21.060
We've got some starter
open-source code

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.060
on GitHub that can help
you connect your Arduino

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:24.175
devices to GCP.

00:01:24.175 --> 00:01:25.800
Check the link below
in the description

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:26.970
for more information.

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:29.820
We also have tons of tutorials
on Google Cloud IRT Core,

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:32.301
including an Internet of
Things QuickStart lab.

00:01:32.301 --> 00:01:34.050
Click on the link in
the description below

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:35.560
to check them out
and get started.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:37.518
We'll catch you next time
on Google Cloud Labs.

00:01:37.518 --> 00:01:40.690
[MUSIC PLAYING]

